diagnosis_text:
The network is fully operational. End-to-end connectivity between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is confirmed with zero packet loss across the 4-hop path: pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1. All five BGP sessions are Established with no drops (connections established: 1, dropped: 0 on every peer), and all routers have correct routing tables installed.

There is one notable structural behavior worth documenting: spine_router_0_0 (AS 65100) receives zero prefixes from super_spine_router_0 (AS 65000). This is caused by standard BGP AS-PATH loop prevention — super_spine's best path for both 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 was learned via spine_router_0_0 itself (AS-PATH contains 65100), so when super_spine re-advertises those routes back to spine_router_0_0, the router correctly rejects them. This is logged explicitly: "DENIED due to: as-path contains our own AS" (2 prefixes). This is expected behavior for a unique-AS CLOS design and causes no actual data-plane problem because spine_router_0_0 learns both leaf subnets directly via its leaf-facing interfaces.

The BGP session uptimes of approximately 10 minutes indicate the lab routers were restarted during the maintenance window. The user's reported outage was the brief BGP reconvergence period following that restart — a transient, expected event that self-resolved within seconds of BGP session re-establishment. No configuration fault exists. No corrective fix is required.
=== END ===